Unlink the guest-memory shm object right after it is created.

rexglue backs guest memory with a 4.8 GB POSIX shm object,
/dev/shm/xenia_memory_<tick count>, and unlinks it only in
CloseFileMappingHandle on a clean shutdown. Every kill, crash or
systemctl stop leaves one behind. Once /dev/shm is full, the next
start hangs or dies with SIGBUS.

Nothing reopens the object by name: every view maps from the
descriptor. So unlink it as soon as ftruncate succeeds. The descriptor
and the mappings keep the memory alive, and the kernel frees it when
the process exits, however it exits.

Upstream report: rexglue/rexglue-sdk#445 (open, no fix yet).
The change follows patches/rexglue-posix-shm-unlink.patch in
Heus-Sueh/midnight-club-la-recomp a3927b8, without its unit test.

--- a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
@@ -417,16 +417,23 @@
     shm_unlink(full_path.c_str());
     return kFileMappingHandleInvalid;
   }
+
+  // The mapping is private to this process and all views are created from the
+  // descriptor below, so it never needs to be reopened by name. Unlink it now
+  // while keeping the descriptor alive. This lets the kernel reclaim the
+  // object after an abort or hard exit instead of leaving multi-gigabyte sparse
+  // xenia_memory_* objects resident in /dev/shm.
+  if (shm_unlink(full_path.c_str()) != 0) {
+    close(ret);
+    return kFileMappingHandleInvalid;
+  }
   return static_cast<FileMappingHandle>(ret);
 #endif
 }
 
 void CloseFileMappingHandle(FileMappingHandle handle, const std::filesystem::path& path) {
+  static_cast<void>(path);
   close(static_cast<int>(handle));
-#if !REX_PLATFORM_ANDROID
-  auto full_path = MakeShmName(path);
-  shm_unlink(full_path.c_str());
-#endif
 }
 
